# claude-web-term.service.template — ttyd web terminal for /term, behind Caddy's
# Google-OAuth gate (infra/caddy/, infra/oauth2-proxy/). Spawns web-term.sh per browser
# connection; ttyd always hands that script a real pty, so it — and anything it execs,
# e.g. fleet/bin/cc — behaves exactly like an interactive SSH session. No direct-exec
# fallback path (cc's other branch, taken only with no pty) is ever reachable from here.
#
# REQUIRED before this unit can start: mask the distro-packaged ttyd.service first — a
# plain `apt install ttyd` ships its own generic unit that also wants port 7681, and
# having both enabled races for the same bind (confirmed on the reference box: `systemctl
# is-enabled ttyd.service` -> masked):
#   sudo systemctl mask --now ttyd.service
#
# Serving model — read before assuming ttyd's own page is what browsers see: this unit's
# ttyd process does NOT answer a plain GET /term or /term/. infra/caddy/Caddyfile.
# template's @termhtml route intercepts those two exact paths ahead of the reverse_proxy
# handler below it and serves the HTML page itself via Caddy's own file_server, from the
# dashboard directory. Only /term/ws (the websocket) and /term/token actually reach this
# ttyd process, through Caddy's reverse_proxy to 127.0.0.1:7681.
#
# -I/--index below points at clipboard-shim.html — NOT a complete, working xterm.js page
# by itself. This repo ships only the small, self-authored clipboard-handling <script>
# (rescued from a live deployment that had it spliced into ttyd's own ~700KB bundled
# index.html — a ttyd build/version artifact this repo does not vendor), so a direct,
# Caddy-bypassing hit on this ttyd process (e.g. an SSH tunnel to 127.0.0.1:7681) sees an
# intentionally minimal page until you've produced the real one — run
# infra/webconsole/make-term-index.sh (one step; the manual splice it automates is still
# documented, as a fallback, in clipboard-shim.html's own header comment). This is a
# documented scope boundary, not a bug: the page ordinary browsers actually get is
# Caddy's file_server copy (see above), not this ttyd process's own -I target.
#
# clipboard.conf (the sibling drop-in file) is an alternate, non-destructive way to add
# this same -I flag onto an ALREADY-DEPLOYED ttyd unit you don't want to hand-edit —
# install ONE of the two, not both; applying that drop-in on top of THIS template just
# re-declares an identical ExecStart=.
#
# Do NOT add ttyd's `-t titleFixed=...` here: it pins document.title once and makes the
# client IGNORE every later title change — which silently defeats the whole tab-naming
# chain (tmux set-titles in infra/os/tmux.conf -> OSC title -> browser tab), leaving N
# /term windows all named alike. Without it the tab reads
# "<session> · <dir> | <command> (<host>)" — the session name first, where the tab strip
# can show it. (Removed from a live deployment 2026-08-08 for exactly this reason.)
#
# `-t rendererType=canvas` turns OFF ttyd's default WebGL renderer, on purpose. Backgrounding
# the iOS PWA loses the GPU context, and ttyd 1.7.4 cannot come back from that: its
# onContextLoss handler calls `webglAddon.dispose()` but never clears `this.webglAddon`,
# while the re-enable path is guarded by `if(!this.webglAddon)` — so WebGL is gone for the
# rest of that page load. What you see on the phone is a garbled screen (rows drawn against
# a stale glyph atlas) decaying into a blank one where only background rectangles paint —
# tmux's green status bar with no text in it was the giveaway. Not recoverable from JS
# either: clearTextureAtlas() on a dead context is a no-op, which is why the ccpaint repaint
# block alone wasn't enough. The canvas renderer has no context to lose, and a purged canvas
# IS recoverable by a repaint, which ccpaint already does on foreground. Upgrade path is one
# word: rendererType=webgl restores the old behaviour, =dom drops canvas too (slowest).
# Requires= the tmux server unit (infra/os/claude-tmux.service.template): the server must
# already exist, in ITS cgroup, before ttyd's first `new-session -AD` — otherwise that call
# forks a server inside THIS unit's cgroup and every restart of ttyd (yours, or needrestart's
# after a security update) kills every live session with it. Learned 2026-08-25, twice.
[Unit]
Description=Web terminal (ttyd) for Claude Code sessions — /term behind Caddy auth
Requires=claude-tmux.service
After=network.target claude-tmux.service

[Service]
Type=simple
User={{ADMIN_USER}}
Environment=LANG=C.UTF-8
ExecStart=/usr/bin/ttyd --port 7681 --interface 127.0.0.1 --base-path /term --writable --url-arg -t fontSize=14 -t rendererType=canvas -I /opt/claude-dashboard/clipboard-shim.html /opt/claude-dashboard/web-term.sh
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=3

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
